## Namespace

The functions/classes in in vhlab-library should live in the namespace vhlib. For example, functions should be vhlib.fun.

## Folder mapping

The Python package does not mirror the MATLAB folder names; the abbreviations
are decoded here and nowhere else, so keep this table current.

| MATLAB folder                            | Python package        |
|------------------------------------------|-----------------------|
| `AnalysisTools/CellDatabaseManagement/`  | `vhlib/CDM/`          |
| `AnalysisTools/MeasuredData/`            | `vhlib/md/`           |
| `StimulusDecoding/`                      | `vhlib/StimDecode/`   |
| `AnalysisTools/ResponsesFitsIndexesPlotting/response_stats/` | `vhlib/response_stats/` |

## Bridge files

Every `vhlib` package carries a `vhlib_matlab_python_bridge.yaml` recording,
for each ported function, where it came from in MATLAB and what was decided
along the way. This is the same convention NDI-python and did-python use
(`ndi_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`, `did_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`); the file
name is `<python package>_matlab_python_bridge.yaml`.

- `vhlib/vhlib_matlab_python_bridge.yaml` — the folder mapping above, the status
  vocabulary, and a per-area decision for every part of vhlab-library-matlab,
  including the parts with no Python presence at all.
- `vhlib/CDM/`, `vhlib/StimDecode/`, `vhlib/md/`, `vhlib/response_stats/` — one
  file each, per function.

Each function entry carries:

- `matlab_path` / `python_path` — the mapping, in both directions. Someone
  looking for the Python home of a MATLAB function, or asking whether it was
  ported at all, should get their answer from these files in one lookup.
- `matlab_last_sync_hash` — the vhlab-library-matlab revision the port was
  checked against, so drift is detectable rather than assumed.
- `input_arguments` / `output_arguments` — with `type_matlab` and `type_python`,
  and `python_name` wherever an argument had to be renamed.
- `decision_log` — where a port deliberately diverges, and why. This is the
  field that stops the same question being re-litigated.
- `status` — one of `ported`, `not_yet_ported`, `not_applicable`,
  `ported_elsewhere`, `deprecated_in_matlab`, `does_not_exist`, defined in
  `status_vocabulary` in the top-level bridge file. `partially_ported` is for
  areas only: a single function that diverges is `ported`, with the divergence
  in its `decision_log`.

When you port a function, add its entry in the same commit. When you decide NOT
to port something, say so with a `not_applicable` or `not_yet_ported` entry and
a reason — otherwise "not ported" and "deliberately skipped" are
indistinguishable, and the next person re-does the research.

`tests/test_bridge.py` enforces the mechanical part: every module has an entry,
every `python_path` exists, every `status` is from the vocabulary.

## Testing

Unittests should be created for all functions/classes.

Run them with:

```bash
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests
```

CI runs that suite and `ruff check vhlib/ tests/` on every push and pull
request. `requirements.txt` installs `vhlab-toolbox-python` straight from
GitHub, since it is not on PyPI.
